---
title: "The 3 signals every B2B account score actually needs"
description: "Most HubSpot scoring models fail because they weight one kind of signal. The useful models combine three — and surface the accounts where all three overlap."
publishDate: 2026-04-18
author: "BoostIdeal"
tags: ["account scoring", "hubspot"]
draft: false
---

Most account scoring breaks the same way: it tracks one kind of signal when it needs three.

Fit alone tells you whether an account *looks like* a customer. It doesn't tell you whether anyone there is paying attention, or whether now is the right moment. Teams that score on fit alone end up with a list of pretty prospects that don't move.

Intent alone is worse. Accounts that are "hot" this week might not be worth pursuing at all. An intent signal from an account that doesn't match your ICP is just noise with a fresh timestamp.

Timing alone — meaning renewal dates, funding events, org chart changes — is the least useful in isolation. It flags when something *could* happen, but not whether it *should*.

The useful model is all three, weighted against each other.

**Fit.** How well does this account match the shape of your best customers? Industry, size, revenue, tech stack, geography. This is static-ish; it doesn't change much quarter to quarter.

**Intent.** Is anyone at this account doing something right now that suggests they're in-market? Pricing page visits, demo requests, direct inbound, competitor research. This is ephemeral — it decays fast.

**Timing.** Is there a reason to move this week? A new VP of Sales started. Their contract with a competitor ends in 60 days. They just raised a Series B. This is event-driven.

An account that hits all three — good fit, showing intent, with a timing catalyst — is a top-10 this week. That's the list your reps should open every Monday.

An account with two of three is worth a touch. One of three is a watchlist. Zero is a name in your CRM that doesn't belong in anyone's weekly plan.

The mistake most HubSpot scoring models make: they pick one axis and score the whole database on it. Then reps work off the list, miss the accounts that would have surfaced on a different axis, and eventually go back to gut.

Score on all three. Surface accounts where they overlap. That's the focus your team's been looking for.
